WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.283 --> 00:00:07.700
MARC VANDEMEULEBROECKE: My
name is Marc Vandemeulebroecke.

00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:09.630
I work at Novartis.

00:00:09.630 --> 00:00:11.690
And I just want to
say a few motivating

00:00:11.690 --> 00:00:15.350
words on why we're
doing what we're doing

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:19.180
and what you can
expect to hear today.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:22.270
We start from the inside
that graphics and visuals

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:24.850
are such an important
component of the work we

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:27.770
do as quantitative scientists.

00:00:27.770 --> 00:00:30.880
They're so important for
ourselves to gain insights

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:33.910
into data, as well
as for communicating

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:37.780
results and conclusions
to our stakeholders.

00:00:37.780 --> 00:00:40.990
And if you've been following
all those coronavirus charts out

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:43.900
there for the past
few weeks, like I did,

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:46.510
you will agree that graphics
are key to understanding

00:00:46.510 --> 00:00:49.570
what's going on.

00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:52.720
Unfortunately, however,
we are not always

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:56.650
good at creating
effective graphs.

00:00:56.650 --> 00:00:58.870
How often do we
see or even create

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:01.860
ineffective visualizations?

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:05.890
And do we even know
which choices or features

00:01:05.890 --> 00:01:11.620
make a graph effective for a
particular purpose or audience?

00:01:11.620 --> 00:01:16.480
This is our first focus to
convey this understanding.

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:19.810
And this point is actually
part of a wider initiative

00:01:19.810 --> 00:01:21.340
beyond today's webinar.

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:23.050
And you will find a
corresponding link

00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:24.175
at the end of these slides.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:30.870
Secondly, once we do know
what makes an effective graph,

00:01:30.870 --> 00:01:33.930
how easily can we
actually implement it?

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:37.840
That is, create it quickly
and with minimal effort.

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:40.170
This is our second focus--

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:42.780
the development of
an R package that

00:01:42.780 --> 00:01:46.170
has good graphical
principles sort of built in,

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:48.780
building upon many of the
great packages out there,

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:51.710
and minimizing the code
required for the user.

00:01:54.370 --> 00:01:57.040
We quickly realized
that these themes

00:01:57.040 --> 00:02:00.700
are of common interest across
Roche, Novartis, and probably

00:02:00.700 --> 00:02:03.820
many other companies
and institutions.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:08.539
So we have started to team
up and tackle them together.

00:02:08.539 --> 00:02:10.570
And one of the
goals today is also

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:16.020
to call for additional
interested contributors, which

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:18.020
leads me to today's agenda.

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:23.600
After this introduction,
my colleague,

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:25.700
Mark Baille from
Novartis, will speak

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:29.870
about the principles of
effective visual communication

00:02:29.870 --> 00:02:32.100
generally.

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:34.890
He will then hand over
to Charlotta Fruchtenicht

00:02:34.890 --> 00:02:37.440
and Diego Saldana
from Roche, who

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:39.900
will focus more
specifically and a bit more

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:45.360
technically on the R package
which we've started to develop.

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:47.610
The goal of this package,
as I said earlier,

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:50.850
is to make it really easy to
implement those good principles

00:02:50.850 --> 00:02:54.930
in practice, specifically in a
health care or drug development

00:02:54.930 --> 00:02:58.830
context, which is where we work.

00:02:58.830 --> 00:03:00.960
All this is work in progress.

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.030
And at the end of
the webinar we will

00:03:03.030 --> 00:03:06.300
call for further contributors,
and we will hopefully also

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:10.480
have a few minutes for Q&amp;amp;A.

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:14.910
So with that, Mark,
do you want to start?

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:17.550
MARK BAILLE: Thank you, Marc.

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:20.340
So effective data
visualization is a key scale

00:03:20.340 --> 00:03:23.700
for all quantitative scientists,
including statisticians,

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:26.100
epidemiologists,
or data scientists,

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.050
whatever your current title is.

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:29.670
Traditionally, this
is a skill that

00:03:29.670 --> 00:03:32.940
has not been a primary focus
in the training and development

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:34.920
of quantitative scientists.

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:36.870
In graduate degrees,
the focus is often

00:03:36.870 --> 00:03:39.900
on analytical and
technical competencies.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:41.970
The skill of visual
communication

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:46.650
is often self-taught, but
develops later on in the job.

00:03:46.650 --> 00:03:50.550
So effective visualization
is extremely important.

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:53.700
It is a key skill that allows
us to convey complex concepts

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:55.650
and information to others.

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:59.070
If we get this right, it helps
support appropriate decisions

00:03:59.070 --> 00:03:59.880
or actions.

00:03:59.880 --> 00:04:01.320
If we get this
wrong, it can lead

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:04.510
to misinformation,
confusion, or even harm,

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:07.320
especially in clinical
and medical research.

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:09.810
The visualization on the
right is a good recent example

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:13.597
of this, covering the
current coronavirus pandemic.

00:04:13.597 --> 00:04:15.930
The visualization was recently
published in The New York

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:16.950
Times.

00:04:16.950 --> 00:04:19.440
It displays a concept
of social distancing

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:21.300
and why it is important.

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:24.750
It is important because it helps
break the transmission chain.

00:04:24.750 --> 00:04:28.570
The graphic also illustrates the
concept of exponential growth.

00:04:28.570 --> 00:04:31.200
This is something we've
heard a lot of recently.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:33.030
Here we see an
infected individual who

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:34.740
goes on to infect two others.

00:04:34.740 --> 00:04:36.750
They in turn infect
two others until we

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:39.945
begin to see many individuals
infected with the virus,

00:04:39.945 --> 00:04:42.470
all stemming from
a single person.

00:04:42.470 --> 00:04:44.340
By practicing
social distancing we

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:46.110
may break this chain,
therefore reducing

00:04:46.110 --> 00:04:48.480
the number of subsequent
infections, which

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:50.160
could be substantial,
as displayed

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:53.200
in the bottom graphic.

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:55.430
We are not always good at it.

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:58.520
On the right is an example
from a clinical study report.

00:04:58.520 --> 00:05:00.200
Waterfall plots
are commonly used

00:05:00.200 --> 00:05:03.780
to report treatment response
in oncology clinical trials.

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:05.840
Aesthetically, this
plot doesn't look good,

00:05:05.840 --> 00:05:09.020
from the choice of patterns
to the general look and feel.

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:11.630
For me, it feels like it was
produced by a typewriter rather

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:12.850
than modern software.

00:05:12.850 --> 00:05:15.050
There is a lot not to
like about this plot.

00:05:15.050 --> 00:05:18.140
But as I said earlier, if we
get visual communication wrong,

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:20.420
it can lead to the
wrong conclusions.

00:05:20.420 --> 00:05:23.130
And then working in medical
and clinical research,

00:05:23.130 --> 00:05:25.100
this can often result in harm.

00:05:25.100 --> 00:05:27.320
So apart from the
aesthetic limitations,

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:30.270
the real failing of this graph
is not the look and feel,

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:31.910
but that the graph
expects the reader

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:34.460
to make calculations
mentally to compare treatment

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:37.040
effects across multiple doses.

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:39.380
Waterfall plots are often
used to support decision

00:05:39.380 --> 00:05:43.790
making, especially in critical
indications found in oncology.

00:05:43.790 --> 00:05:46.790
So asking the reader to make
those calculations mentally

00:05:46.790 --> 00:05:48.850
isn't an effective
way to communicate.

00:05:51.610 --> 00:05:55.160
So what do we mean by effective?

00:05:55.160 --> 00:05:58.540
We don't necessarily
mean beautiful.

00:05:58.540 --> 00:06:02.770
So we don't really mean
to focus on the beautiful.

00:06:02.770 --> 00:06:04.920
So to illustrate
here is an example

00:06:04.920 --> 00:06:08.290
of a visually appealing
graph from David McCandless.

00:06:08.290 --> 00:06:11.580
He is a data artist and
journalist from the UK.

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:15.120
The visualization is called
Mountains out of Molehills:

00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:17.950
a Timeline of Global
Media Scare Stories.

00:06:17.950 --> 00:06:19.750
The visualization
attempts to investigate

00:06:19.750 --> 00:06:21.720
the number of news
story reports,

00:06:21.720 --> 00:06:24.100
and if there is a
sensible reporting.

00:06:24.100 --> 00:06:27.330
And so this could cause
unnecessary panic.

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:29.910
It covers earlier
pandemics such as SARS,

00:06:29.910 --> 00:06:34.590
bird flu, and swine flu, also
video games and killer wasps.

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:37.170
Visually it is appealing
and draws you in.

00:06:37.170 --> 00:06:39.150
But I would argue
it's not effective,

00:06:39.150 --> 00:06:42.210
as key information is
hidden away in the legend.

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:43.740
So if you look at
the legend, there

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:46.950
are numbers and brackets that
provide the deaths associated

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:47.760
with each story.

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:50.940
It's very difficult
to see, I can imagine.

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:53.640
This information is important
to determine if there really

00:06:53.640 --> 00:06:54.900
is over-reporting.

00:06:54.900 --> 00:06:57.150
But the information is not
factored into the main body

00:06:57.150 --> 00:06:58.380
of the graph.

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:00.450
Also, the subtitle
uses languages

00:07:00.450 --> 00:07:03.510
such as scare stories,
it has comparisons

00:07:03.510 --> 00:07:07.560
to annual seasonal flu deaths,
and it also has comparisons

00:07:07.560 --> 00:07:10.020
to topics such as video gaming.

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:12.270
All of these design
choices indirectly

00:07:12.270 --> 00:07:17.020
influence the reader, which can
then influence decision making.

00:07:17.020 --> 00:07:18.380
So beautiful and effective.

00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:19.750
That's what we really want.

00:07:19.750 --> 00:07:22.570
So here is an example of a
recent effective visualization

00:07:22.570 --> 00:07:25.000
published by The Economist.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:26.860
The visualization
captures a recent phrase

00:07:26.860 --> 00:07:29.990
that we hear a lot of
now-- flatten the curve.

00:07:29.990 --> 00:07:32.830
The graph summarizes
this concept really well.

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:34.750
So we have two
distributions of a time,

00:07:34.750 --> 00:07:38.020
which represent the number of
infections in the population.

00:07:38.020 --> 00:07:40.870
Each distribution
represents a strategy.

00:07:40.870 --> 00:07:43.690
Strategy one for not taking
preventative measures

00:07:43.690 --> 00:07:47.950
such as social distancing,
and strategy two, it does.

00:07:47.950 --> 00:07:49.640
You can see that
strategy two reduces

00:07:49.640 --> 00:07:52.810
the peak of the distribution
through social distancing.

00:07:52.810 --> 00:07:54.760
This strategy
extends the epidemic

00:07:54.760 --> 00:07:57.420
over a longer period
of time, but reduces

00:07:57.420 --> 00:07:59.290
the peak number of infections.

00:07:59.290 --> 00:08:02.170
The spatial visualization
is effective in introducing

00:08:02.170 --> 00:08:05.560
this concept of
flattening the curve.

00:08:05.560 --> 00:08:07.810
But can we be even
more effective?

00:08:07.810 --> 00:08:10.440
So here is another
version of the same graph.

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:13.710
Aesthetically, it's not as
appealing as the previous one,

00:08:13.710 --> 00:08:16.650
but by adding a dotted line
and a simple annotation--

00:08:16.650 --> 00:08:20.100
Healthcare System Capacity--
this specialization now

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:21.840
highlights the why.

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:24.240
Why do we want to flatten
an infection curve

00:08:24.240 --> 00:08:28.260
and spread an epidemic over
a longer period of time?

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:29.940
The purpose of
flattening the curve

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:32.640
is not to overwhelm
the health care system.

00:08:32.640 --> 00:08:35.159
So the dotted line illustrates
that the first strategy

00:08:35.159 --> 00:08:37.350
would result in more
infections than the health care

00:08:37.350 --> 00:08:38.780
system could cope with.

00:08:38.780 --> 00:08:41.640
This, sadly, can have
terrible consequences

00:08:41.640 --> 00:08:43.659
that we are currently
witnessing now.

00:08:43.659 --> 00:08:45.660
So both graphs are
very effective ways

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:48.960
to convey complex
concepts to the population

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:50.980
and getting messages out there.

00:08:50.980 --> 00:08:53.310
And I just want to highlight
that both of these slides

00:08:53.310 --> 00:08:55.680
were influenced by a
tweet from [INAUDIBLE]..

00:08:55.680 --> 00:08:59.100
The link is there if you
want to follow up on it.

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:03.690
So effective visualization is
effective visual communication.

00:09:03.690 --> 00:09:07.830
Effective graphs are visually
appealing, intuitive, legible,

00:09:07.830 --> 00:09:10.830
and use correct graph
type and axis scales,

00:09:10.830 --> 00:09:14.350
and use proximity and alignment
to facilitate comparisons,

00:09:14.350 --> 00:09:17.460
and use labels and annotations
to add clarity to the message,

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:19.237
as I illustrated before.

00:09:19.237 --> 00:09:20.820
And more importantly,
effective use of

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:24.150
visualizations enable clear
and impactful communication,

00:09:24.150 --> 00:09:26.760
evaluate our influence
with our stakeholders,

00:09:26.760 --> 00:09:29.742
and facilitate informed
decision making.

00:09:29.742 --> 00:09:31.200
This is one of the
main motivations

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:33.420
behind the collaboration
that we are presenting today

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:37.656
and the initial design
of the visR package.

00:09:37.656 --> 00:09:40.430
But how do we become
more effective?

00:09:40.430 --> 00:09:41.900
Myself and colleagues
at Novartis

00:09:41.900 --> 00:09:44.870
have been thinking about this
topic for a number of years.

00:09:44.870 --> 00:09:46.310
We've tried a
number of approaches

00:09:46.310 --> 00:09:48.530
to try and address this
question from reading

00:09:48.530 --> 00:09:51.710
all the literature available,
defining best practices,

00:09:51.710 --> 00:09:54.590
developing galleries
of codes, newsletters

00:09:54.590 --> 00:09:56.560
to highlight the
importance of the topic,

00:09:56.560 --> 00:09:58.340
to handing out
cheat sheets with--

00:09:58.340 --> 00:10:01.160
and we've had various
levels of success.

00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:04.220
But to make this concept stick
and to be easier to remember,

00:10:04.220 --> 00:10:07.310
we recently defined what we call
the Three Laws for Effective

00:10:07.310 --> 00:10:08.870
Visual Communication--

00:10:08.870 --> 00:10:11.990
have a clear purpose,
show the data clearly,

00:10:11.990 --> 00:10:13.700
and make the message obvious.

00:10:13.700 --> 00:10:16.890
You can think of these
three laws as superheroes.

00:10:16.890 --> 00:10:18.600
So have a clear purpose.

00:10:18.600 --> 00:10:20.820
This is really about why
do we need this graph?

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:24.390
So you should really know the
purpose of creating the graph.

00:10:24.390 --> 00:10:27.930
And this is really related
also to data analysis itself.

00:10:27.930 --> 00:10:30.020
We should identify the
quantitative evidence

00:10:30.020 --> 00:10:33.290
to support the purpose and
also identify the audience

00:10:33.290 --> 00:10:36.800
and focus the design and
to support their needs.

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:38.000
Show the data clearly.

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:40.780
This is about avoiding
misrepresentation.

00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:43.960
Choosing the appropriate graph
type to display the data,

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:46.130
maximizing data to ink ratio.

00:10:46.130 --> 00:10:49.760
So this is really just a complex
way of saying, keep it simple.

00:10:49.760 --> 00:10:51.860
Finally, make the
message obvious.

00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:55.330
So using proximity and
alignment to aid in comparisons,

00:10:55.330 --> 00:10:57.522
minimize mental arithmetic.

00:10:57.522 --> 00:10:59.480
This is really going back
to the waterfall plot

00:10:59.480 --> 00:11:01.063
where you really
don't want the reader

00:11:01.063 --> 00:11:03.080
to be doing summations
in their head.

00:11:03.080 --> 00:11:07.230
And using colors and annotations
to highlight important details.

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:09.200
So I'll now go through
each of these three laws

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:11.900
in more detail.

00:11:11.900 --> 00:11:13.525
So Law 1-- Have a Clear Purpose.

00:11:17.100 --> 00:11:20.150
This first law is really
advanced common sense.

00:11:20.150 --> 00:11:23.050
It follows typical planning
questions such as why, what,

00:11:23.050 --> 00:11:24.250
who, and where.

00:11:24.250 --> 00:11:26.060
So why do I need a graph?

00:11:26.060 --> 00:11:27.460
And what is its purpose?

00:11:27.460 --> 00:11:30.610
Is it to explore data
or to deliver a message?

00:11:30.610 --> 00:11:33.610
What evidence is available
to support this purpose?

00:11:33.610 --> 00:11:36.580
This could be data sets, new
experiments, simulations,

00:11:36.580 --> 00:11:39.450
for example.

00:11:39.450 --> 00:11:39.970
OK.

00:11:39.970 --> 00:11:41.395
Who is my intended audience?

00:11:41.395 --> 00:11:44.470
Is it a mix of specialists
and non-specialists, or both?

00:11:44.470 --> 00:11:46.810
You really need to think
about who this audience is

00:11:46.810 --> 00:11:48.460
and support their needs.

00:11:48.460 --> 00:11:49.090
And the where.

00:11:49.090 --> 00:11:50.260
Where will I publish?

00:11:50.260 --> 00:11:52.340
And what other
constraints they are?

00:11:52.340 --> 00:11:55.210
So for the where, we may
identify certain formats

00:11:55.210 --> 00:11:58.930
such as a Word document,
a PowerPoint presentation,

00:11:58.930 --> 00:12:02.110
R Markdown notebook,
or a shiny app.

00:12:02.110 --> 00:12:05.800
And really, the design and
the purpose and the principles

00:12:05.800 --> 00:12:09.145
that we have really,
really is important for all

00:12:09.145 --> 00:12:10.270
of these different formats.

00:12:10.270 --> 00:12:12.640
But depending on the format,
some of these principles

00:12:12.640 --> 00:12:15.700
may apply and the
design choices may vary.

00:12:19.120 --> 00:12:21.418
Another aspect to this
law is to identify

00:12:21.418 --> 00:12:22.960
what are the underlying
questions you

00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:24.410
are trying to address.

00:12:24.410 --> 00:12:27.640
So here I highlighted
an article by Roger Peng

00:12:27.640 --> 00:12:30.880
that discusses his thoughts on
John Tukey, exploratory data

00:12:30.880 --> 00:12:33.580
analysis, and design thinking.

00:12:33.580 --> 00:12:35.620
He argues that better
analyses are related

00:12:35.620 --> 00:12:37.510
to the quality of the question.

00:12:37.510 --> 00:12:39.910
So more time spent on
defining better questions

00:12:39.910 --> 00:12:41.790
is time well spent.

00:12:41.790 --> 00:12:44.350
And this is related to
the famous Tukey quote--

00:12:44.350 --> 00:12:46.300
"An approximate answer
to the right question

00:12:46.300 --> 00:12:48.460
is worth a great deal
more than a precise answer

00:12:48.460 --> 00:12:50.170
to the wrong question."

00:12:50.170 --> 00:12:52.030
So the message here
is to spend more time

00:12:52.030 --> 00:12:54.495
thinking about your question
prior to an analysis

00:12:54.495 --> 00:12:59.160
and designing the visualization.

00:12:59.160 --> 00:13:01.560
Another aspect to this
law is why are you

00:13:01.560 --> 00:13:03.150
creating visualization?

00:13:03.150 --> 00:13:04.680
So thinking about
this aspect can

00:13:04.680 --> 00:13:06.810
help identify the
audience and their needs,

00:13:06.810 --> 00:13:09.240
which in turn helps
with the design.

00:13:09.240 --> 00:13:12.030
So for example, if you
want to dig deep into data,

00:13:12.030 --> 00:13:15.090
get familiar with the data,
or find the story in my data,

00:13:15.090 --> 00:13:17.520
the audience is normally you.

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:19.140
If you want to
communicate the results

00:13:19.140 --> 00:13:21.060
or to tell the story
behind the data,

00:13:21.060 --> 00:13:23.260
then the audience
is someone else.

00:13:23.260 --> 00:13:26.460
So being clear on the
purpose is important here.

00:13:26.460 --> 00:13:28.590
For example, if you're
designing the visualization

00:13:28.590 --> 00:13:30.930
to be presented to a
group of stakeholders

00:13:30.930 --> 00:13:34.470
where you have 3 minutes to get
your recommendations across,

00:13:34.470 --> 00:13:38.010
do you really want the
audience to play Where's Wally?

00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:41.300
Or in the case of
America, Where's Waldo?

00:13:41.300 --> 00:13:43.230
And if you're really
interested, Wally

00:13:43.230 --> 00:13:47.670
is in the middle of
the picture here.

00:13:47.670 --> 00:13:49.455
Law 2, Show the Data Clearly.

00:13:52.350 --> 00:13:55.980
The key part of this law is
not to lie or represent data.

00:13:55.980 --> 00:13:58.770
Try to be open and
transparent of your analysis

00:13:58.770 --> 00:14:00.870
and the reporting of it.

00:14:00.870 --> 00:14:03.540
Here we have an example from
YouGov on the important topic

00:14:03.540 --> 00:14:05.160
of pizza toppings.

00:14:05.160 --> 00:14:08.090
The visualization displays
a pizza pie chart.

00:14:08.090 --> 00:14:11.760
The visualization is confusing,
as the numbers add up to way

00:14:11.760 --> 00:14:13.950
beyond 100%.

00:14:13.950 --> 00:14:15.510
Another thing
that's confusing is

00:14:15.510 --> 00:14:18.360
mushroom as the UK'S
most liked pizza topping.

00:14:18.360 --> 00:14:20.890
So as a Scotsman, I really
find this hard to believe.

00:14:23.430 --> 00:14:26.297
Another aspect of this
law is to be transparent.

00:14:26.297 --> 00:14:28.130
Here I have displayed
a response from YouGov

00:14:28.130 --> 00:14:31.020
to clarify the purpose
of their chart.

00:14:31.020 --> 00:14:33.010
The intent was not to
display a pie chart,

00:14:33.010 --> 00:14:35.730
but to present
percentages from a survey.

00:14:35.730 --> 00:14:38.790
Here we see that being clear
and transparent builds trust,

00:14:38.790 --> 00:14:41.130
and it helps with communication.

00:14:41.130 --> 00:14:43.112
Another aspect to
this apology is

00:14:43.112 --> 00:14:44.820
it highlights the need
to think carefully

00:14:44.820 --> 00:14:46.710
about your design
and the graph type.

00:14:50.070 --> 00:14:53.270
So choosing the correct graph
type aids in interpretation.

00:14:53.270 --> 00:14:55.020
So thinking about the
purpose of the graph

00:14:55.020 --> 00:14:58.470
can help identify
appropriate graph types.

00:14:58.470 --> 00:15:01.230
Often we don't need to
reinvent the wheel here.

00:15:01.230 --> 00:15:04.350
For example, if you want to
show a deviation, a correlation,

00:15:04.350 --> 00:15:06.570
or a ranking, then there
are common graph types

00:15:06.570 --> 00:15:07.590
that we can select from.

00:15:10.250 --> 00:15:13.160
Another aspect to think
about in terms of graph type

00:15:13.160 --> 00:15:18.660
is what information
do you want to convey?

00:15:18.660 --> 00:15:20.810
So here's an example
of the same data

00:15:20.810 --> 00:15:23.170
displayed six different ways.

00:15:23.170 --> 00:15:26.000
The pie and donut
charts in panels A and B

00:15:26.000 --> 00:15:28.250
make it difficult to see
the order of magnitude

00:15:28.250 --> 00:15:30.020
of some of the segments.

00:15:30.020 --> 00:15:32.720
They're only to compare
areas or angles.

00:15:32.720 --> 00:15:35.380
These attributes are
not easy to compare.

00:15:35.380 --> 00:15:37.430
The donut chart even
omits the angles

00:15:37.430 --> 00:15:39.920
and bends the areas to make
it more challenging to make

00:15:39.920 --> 00:15:42.000
these comparisons.

00:15:42.000 --> 00:15:45.300
The mosaic plot in Panel
C only relies on areas.

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:48.180
Again, it's hard to
compare across categories.

00:15:48.180 --> 00:15:50.870
And it's better to use
lengths with a common baseline

00:15:50.870 --> 00:15:53.210
or possessions on a common
scale, such as a bar chart

00:15:53.210 --> 00:15:54.420
or dot plot.

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:56.370
The bar chart in
Panel D, though,

00:15:56.370 --> 00:15:59.160
introduces a fake dimension,
which is unnecessary

00:15:59.160 --> 00:16:01.500
and makes it hard to
compare numerical values

00:16:01.500 --> 00:16:03.590
from the height of the bars.

00:16:03.590 --> 00:16:07.320
Panels E and F are simple
and show the data clearly.

00:16:07.320 --> 00:16:09.260
They also order the
data by magnitude

00:16:09.260 --> 00:16:10.860
to aid comparison even further.

00:16:10.860 --> 00:16:13.680
So you're really not
making the reader work

00:16:13.680 --> 00:16:17.370
or do the mental
arithmetic in their heads.

00:16:17.370 --> 00:16:19.980
The dot plot in Panel
F uses minimal ink

00:16:19.980 --> 00:16:21.985
and draws eye to the
position of the dots.

00:16:21.985 --> 00:16:23.610
So it's probably the
most effective way

00:16:23.610 --> 00:16:24.690
of displaying the data.

00:16:27.610 --> 00:16:29.690
Choose the right
scale for your data.

00:16:29.690 --> 00:16:32.440
So try to avoid plotting
log-normally distributed

00:16:32.440 --> 00:16:34.010
variables on a linear scale.

00:16:34.010 --> 00:16:36.010
This can lead to
misleading interpretation,

00:16:36.010 --> 00:16:40.160
especially when you introduce
lines and code uncertainty.

00:16:40.160 --> 00:16:42.070
So in this example,
the uncertainty

00:16:42.070 --> 00:16:45.040
appears to increase
above 1, which is unity.

00:16:45.040 --> 00:16:47.680
But this is an artifact of
the choice of the scale.

00:16:47.680 --> 00:16:49.600
Using a log scale
resolves this issue.

00:16:52.180 --> 00:16:55.450
Another important
aspect related to this

00:16:55.450 --> 00:16:57.920
is time and how
that is displayed.

00:16:57.920 --> 00:17:00.640
So space measurements
proportional

00:17:00.640 --> 00:17:03.234
to the time they were measured.

00:17:03.234 --> 00:17:04.359
So what do we mean by that?

00:17:04.359 --> 00:17:06.401
So measurements that are
displayed close together

00:17:06.401 --> 00:17:08.470
are perceived to
be closer in time.

00:17:08.470 --> 00:17:10.300
Not reflecting this
in your visualization

00:17:10.300 --> 00:17:12.430
could introduce a visual bias.

00:17:12.430 --> 00:17:14.349
So for example,
slopes or trajectories

00:17:14.349 --> 00:17:17.950
may appear steeper or less
so than they typically are.

00:17:20.569 --> 00:17:21.609
Law 3.

00:17:21.609 --> 00:17:24.270
Make the Message Obvious.

00:17:24.270 --> 00:17:27.433
A key aspect of this law is to
assume that the reader will--

00:17:27.433 --> 00:17:28.850
not to assume that
the reader will

00:17:28.850 --> 00:17:30.892
understand what message
is the information you're

00:17:30.892 --> 00:17:32.720
trying to convey.

00:17:32.720 --> 00:17:34.340
You have to really
work at it to get

00:17:34.340 --> 00:17:36.080
them to understand
this information

00:17:36.080 --> 00:17:38.000
or what you're trying
to convey to them.

00:17:40.510 --> 00:17:43.540
A simple illustration of this
is to try and not to set text

00:17:43.540 --> 00:17:44.410
at an angle.

00:17:44.410 --> 00:17:47.290
Think of alternatives such
as transposing the graph.

00:17:47.290 --> 00:17:49.300
So the left-hand plot,
we are essentially

00:17:49.300 --> 00:17:52.180
asking the reader to tilt
their head to read the text.

00:17:52.180 --> 00:17:55.360
Do we really want to
give readers neck strain?

00:17:55.360 --> 00:17:56.830
A simple transposing
of the graph

00:17:56.830 --> 00:17:59.560
allows us to resolve this
issue and also introduces

00:17:59.560 --> 00:18:02.460
a more effective visualization.

00:18:02.460 --> 00:18:04.410
Also, try to avoid
unnecessary color.

00:18:07.530 --> 00:18:09.750
So avoid using color
to differentiate

00:18:09.750 --> 00:18:11.820
between categories
of the same variable.

00:18:11.820 --> 00:18:14.190
This can introduce
confusion in a similar way

00:18:14.190 --> 00:18:17.580
to the previous pizza
example from YouGov.

00:18:17.580 --> 00:18:20.660
Also, introducing unnecessary
color when it is not required

00:18:20.660 --> 00:18:25.990
limits us to when we really
do need to use color later on.

00:18:25.990 --> 00:18:28.920
For example, use color
when it adds value.

00:18:28.920 --> 00:18:31.260
Use a bold, saturated,
or contrasting color

00:18:31.260 --> 00:18:33.510
to emphasize important details.

00:18:33.510 --> 00:18:35.910
So the left-hand plot,
our eyes are naturally

00:18:35.910 --> 00:18:37.830
drawn to the top of the bar.

00:18:37.830 --> 00:18:41.210
The right-hand plot, we
introduce a contrasting color.

00:18:41.210 --> 00:18:43.950
Now our eyes are drawn to
the bottom of the bars.

00:18:43.950 --> 00:18:45.450
This is an important
design choice

00:18:45.450 --> 00:18:47.940
that allows us to
highlight key information

00:18:47.940 --> 00:18:52.100
and can be used to really
highlight important messaging.

00:18:52.100 --> 00:18:54.940
And here's an example of
using annotations and labels

00:18:54.940 --> 00:18:56.430
to help with messages.

00:18:56.430 --> 00:18:58.960
So use labels and
annotations to tell stories

00:18:58.960 --> 00:19:01.060
about the data and models.

00:19:01.060 --> 00:19:05.080
On the left-hand side we have
a typical dose response plot.

00:19:05.080 --> 00:19:08.322
By adding annotations such as
Active Control and Target Dose

00:19:08.322 --> 00:19:10.780
on the right-hand side, we
start to provide more contextual

00:19:10.780 --> 00:19:12.040
information for the reader.

00:19:12.040 --> 00:19:14.380
And then we can start to get
to the "why" of the plot.

00:19:14.380 --> 00:19:17.140
So think back to the
flattening of the curve plot

00:19:17.140 --> 00:19:20.320
where we add in the dotted line
to indicate the health care

00:19:20.320 --> 00:19:23.460
system capacity.

00:19:23.460 --> 00:19:25.170
So finally, here's
an example that we

00:19:25.170 --> 00:19:27.090
created of a
visualization that tries

00:19:27.090 --> 00:19:29.230
to follow all three principles.

00:19:29.230 --> 00:19:33.060
The analysis is as a subgroup
comparison of a genetic marker.

00:19:33.060 --> 00:19:35.910
The analysis is a repeated
measures model over time.

00:19:35.910 --> 00:19:39.000
We use the title to indicate
a conclusion or recommendation

00:19:39.000 --> 00:19:40.140
from the analysis.

00:19:40.140 --> 00:19:41.910
So Genetic marker
positive is not

00:19:41.910 --> 00:19:44.430
predictive of
treatment response.

00:19:44.430 --> 00:19:47.070
We use annotations to indicate
the direction of the treatment

00:19:47.070 --> 00:19:48.680
benefit.

00:19:48.680 --> 00:19:52.440
And we also highlight
the main time point,

00:19:52.440 --> 00:19:57.600
which is our primary analysis
at week 12 with a dotted circle.

00:19:57.600 --> 00:19:59.460
Instead of legends,
we use annotations

00:19:59.460 --> 00:20:00.840
to indicate each subgroup.

00:20:00.840 --> 00:20:02.580
And we also use color
to differentiate

00:20:02.580 --> 00:20:05.480
between subgroups.

00:20:05.480 --> 00:20:07.190
So if you're interested
to learn more

00:20:07.190 --> 00:20:09.830
about the three laws and some of
the principles I've introduced

00:20:09.830 --> 00:20:12.230
today, I'd recommend that
you visit the website

00:20:12.230 --> 00:20:15.148
below--
graphicprinciples.github.io.

00:20:15.148 --> 00:20:16.940
We have a cheat sheet
there that summarizes

00:20:16.940 --> 00:20:18.530
a lot of these principles.

00:20:18.530 --> 00:20:21.343
And it's developed using R.

00:20:21.343 --> 00:20:23.260
There are also other
great resources out there

00:20:23.260 --> 00:20:24.280
that you can use.

00:20:24.280 --> 00:20:28.030
For example, the book
by Jean-luc Doumont

00:20:28.030 --> 00:20:30.546
really introduces
scientific communication.

00:20:30.546 --> 00:20:33.860
And it's really an example of a
book that everyone should read.

00:20:33.860 --> 00:20:36.190
If you're really interested
in data visualization,

00:20:36.190 --> 00:20:39.160
I would recommend the other two
books, the Data Visualization

00:20:39.160 --> 00:20:41.200
from Kieran Healy,
and also the book

00:20:41.200 --> 00:20:43.900
by Claus Wilke, if you
wish to focus on not only

00:20:43.900 --> 00:20:46.450
just the principles
of data visualization,

00:20:46.450 --> 00:20:50.730
but also how to do this in R.

00:20:50.730 --> 00:20:53.070
So effective data
visualization is

00:20:53.070 --> 00:20:55.080
effective visual communication.

00:20:55.080 --> 00:20:57.690
Effective visualizations
enable clear and impactful

00:20:57.690 --> 00:21:01.950
communication, they evaluate
our influence with stakeholders,

00:21:01.950 --> 00:21:04.770
and they facilitate
informed decision making.

00:21:04.770 --> 00:21:06.990
To help design
effective visualizations

00:21:06.990 --> 00:21:08.460
remember the three laws--

00:21:08.460 --> 00:21:11.680
purpose, clarity, and message.

00:21:11.680 --> 00:21:15.120
So typically now I
would do a handover that

00:21:15.120 --> 00:21:18.540
would require either a strong
or a moderate handshake,

00:21:18.540 --> 00:21:22.290
possibly a prolonged high five,
maybe just a normal high five,

00:21:22.290 --> 00:21:23.580
or a prolonged fist bump.

00:21:23.580 --> 00:21:25.570
But given the current
situation, I'll

00:21:25.570 --> 00:21:28.030
just do a virtual fist
bump to Charlotta.

00:21:28.030 --> 00:21:29.820
Charlotta.

00:21:29.820 --> 00:21:31.860
CHARLOTTA FRUCHTENICHT:
Thank you, Mark.

00:21:31.860 --> 00:21:33.830
So what you've seen
in this first section

00:21:33.830 --> 00:21:37.280
is how important it is to
implement graphics principles

00:21:37.280 --> 00:21:40.700
for effective visual
communication.

00:21:40.700 --> 00:21:43.150
But the reason why we
are giving this webinar

00:21:43.150 --> 00:21:46.130
is that you often don't see
that in typical data science

00:21:46.130 --> 00:21:47.510
analyses.

00:21:47.510 --> 00:21:50.150
And the reason for that
is that implementing

00:21:50.150 --> 00:21:52.670
those visual
principles can be quite

00:21:52.670 --> 00:21:55.400
tedious and
time-consuming and adding

00:21:55.400 --> 00:21:58.400
quite a bit of extra
overhead on the coding side.

00:21:58.400 --> 00:22:01.130
So especially early
on in project,

00:22:01.130 --> 00:22:04.460
data scientists tend to skip
a lot of those principles

00:22:04.460 --> 00:22:07.237
to just get on
with their project.

00:22:07.237 --> 00:22:08.820
For example, if you
look on the right,

00:22:08.820 --> 00:22:11.930
you can see that creating this
relatively simple dose response

00:22:11.930 --> 00:22:14.210
curve, that's not even
adhering to all the principles

00:22:14.210 --> 00:22:16.850
that Mark just described.

00:22:16.850 --> 00:22:21.200
And you see that actually it
requires as much extra code

00:22:21.200 --> 00:22:24.050
to style the diagram and
add annotations as it

00:22:24.050 --> 00:22:27.480
does to plot the actual data.

00:22:27.480 --> 00:22:32.270
So because of this extra
work, we often skip it.

00:22:32.270 --> 00:22:33.980
But then downstream,
for example,

00:22:33.980 --> 00:22:37.100
when communicating to
external stakeholders,

00:22:37.100 --> 00:22:40.250
we have to keep
up with that work

00:22:40.250 --> 00:22:43.490
and actually implement
new versions of the plots

00:22:43.490 --> 00:22:45.770
and figures and tables
that we did early

00:22:45.770 --> 00:22:48.930
on exploratory projects.

00:22:48.930 --> 00:22:51.990
So we thought this is
something we'd probably

00:22:51.990 --> 00:22:55.230
have to change if we want to
be more effective in the way we

00:22:55.230 --> 00:22:56.640
communicate our results.

00:22:56.640 --> 00:23:00.500
And if you go to the
next slide, Mark.

00:23:00.500 --> 00:23:02.860
There is actually
an additional point.

00:23:02.860 --> 00:23:06.970
And as Mark mentioned, there
are different stakeholders

00:23:06.970 --> 00:23:11.470
or different audience to
your reporting outcomes.

00:23:11.470 --> 00:23:14.050
So early on in a project
this might be just for you

00:23:14.050 --> 00:23:15.600
and, like, yourself.

00:23:15.600 --> 00:23:18.430
And you might remember all the
work you've done, all the data

00:23:18.430 --> 00:23:21.460
manipulations, and you
might not need a lot

00:23:21.460 --> 00:23:22.690
of additional annotations.

00:23:22.690 --> 00:23:25.750
But usually you're working
in a team, at least

00:23:25.750 --> 00:23:27.620
in clinical development.

00:23:27.620 --> 00:23:30.430
And so you have
additional stakeholders

00:23:30.430 --> 00:23:33.150
that might look at the
outputs that you create,

00:23:33.150 --> 00:23:35.740
maybe figures of tables.

00:23:35.740 --> 00:23:38.230
And you might actually
have those stakeholders

00:23:38.230 --> 00:23:41.890
take those outputs and then
further use them to communicate

00:23:41.890 --> 00:23:44.810
their insights in their teams.

00:23:44.810 --> 00:23:47.860
So it's actually quite important
that whatever you create

00:23:47.860 --> 00:23:51.490
in your analyses cannot
be taken out of context,

00:23:51.490 --> 00:23:55.480
and that at any time it
is possible to reproduce

00:23:55.480 --> 00:23:58.210
the output that you created--
tables and figures--

00:23:58.210 --> 00:24:01.500
and also trace where
the data came from.

00:24:01.500 --> 00:24:04.630
So on the right, you can
see a typical table shell

00:24:04.630 --> 00:24:07.870
for baseline demographics
in the cohort.

00:24:07.870 --> 00:24:09.383
And as you can
see clearly, we're

00:24:09.383 --> 00:24:11.800
showing a table here because
Mark showed a lot of figures,

00:24:11.800 --> 00:24:14.400
and we want to make sure that
you all are aware that outputs

00:24:14.400 --> 00:24:16.250
can also be tables.

00:24:16.250 --> 00:24:18.490
So if you have a
table you can see

00:24:18.490 --> 00:24:20.980
that there is a lot of
metadata in addition

00:24:20.980 --> 00:24:24.580
to the actual demographics
that are being annotated.

00:24:24.580 --> 00:24:28.810
So for example, every table
and figure should have a title.

00:24:28.810 --> 00:24:30.990
And it should name the
data source and the version

00:24:30.990 --> 00:24:32.660
the data comes from.

00:24:32.660 --> 00:24:36.490
So if that plot or that table
finds its way into a PowerPoint

00:24:36.490 --> 00:24:40.330
presentation, it's easy to
trace back where it came from

00:24:40.330 --> 00:24:44.400
and at what time point
the analysis was done.

00:24:44.400 --> 00:24:47.820
We should always have also
as part of the rendered

00:24:47.820 --> 00:24:51.210
object the information about the
abbreviations and table columns

00:24:51.210 --> 00:24:53.190
that are being used, the
statistical tests that

00:24:53.190 --> 00:24:55.850
were employed, as well as
some baseline information

00:24:55.850 --> 00:25:00.030
or basic information about the
cohort, such as sample size.

00:25:00.030 --> 00:25:03.270
Generally, also we would like to
have the reports that we create

00:25:03.270 --> 00:25:06.900
to be harmonized across the
different visualizations--

00:25:06.900 --> 00:25:09.090
plots and figures again--

00:25:09.090 --> 00:25:12.780
so that, for example,
the same treatment

00:25:12.780 --> 00:25:16.200
is always coded by the same
color in different plots.

00:25:16.200 --> 00:25:19.230
So there is this second
layer in addition

00:25:19.230 --> 00:25:22.530
to visual communication and
those graphics principles

00:25:22.530 --> 00:25:24.660
that Mark just
introduced that we often

00:25:24.660 --> 00:25:29.160
have to take into consideration
in our data science project.

00:25:29.160 --> 00:25:31.800
And again, looking
at the metadata,

00:25:31.800 --> 00:25:35.760
you can imagine that annotating
all of this information

00:25:35.760 --> 00:25:38.840
can create quite a bit of
overhead on the coding side.

00:25:38.840 --> 00:25:43.068
And these are often skipped
early on in projects.

00:25:43.068 --> 00:25:44.360
So if you go to the next slide.

00:25:47.590 --> 00:25:50.540
So we realize this is
something we have to change.

00:25:50.540 --> 00:25:52.430
And as Mark said
in the beginning,

00:25:52.430 --> 00:25:56.210
it's probably an issue
that not only data science

00:25:56.210 --> 00:25:58.220
teams at Roche
and Novartis have,

00:25:58.220 --> 00:26:02.030
but also a lot of other
people doing analytics work

00:26:02.030 --> 00:26:04.650
across the industry and beyond.

00:26:04.650 --> 00:26:06.700
So we thought we want
to implement something

00:26:06.700 --> 00:26:08.900
like a tool box that
helps us integrate

00:26:08.900 --> 00:26:13.630
graphical principles in our data
science and analytics projects.

00:26:13.630 --> 00:26:16.000
We didn't want to do
that in a vacuum, though.

00:26:16.000 --> 00:26:19.510
So we reached out to not
only other data scientists

00:26:19.510 --> 00:26:23.200
in our own teams, but also
important stakeholders

00:26:23.200 --> 00:26:26.290
like therapeutic area scientists
and clinical scientists

00:26:26.290 --> 00:26:28.360
to better understand
what they would

00:26:28.360 --> 00:26:32.290
like to see in typical
reports that we create

00:26:32.290 --> 00:26:36.280
and what the crucial aspects are
that should never be missing.

00:26:36.280 --> 00:26:39.100
And with that we came up
with a bunch of development

00:26:39.100 --> 00:26:43.270
considerations that the tool
box we wanted to develop

00:26:43.270 --> 00:26:45.570
should always adhere to.

00:26:45.570 --> 00:26:48.970
And the first one is really
not creating extra work.

00:26:48.970 --> 00:26:52.080
So it should integrate
seamlessly into our analytics

00:26:52.080 --> 00:26:53.750
and reporting workflows.

00:26:53.750 --> 00:26:57.840
And it should work well with
the tools we are already using.

00:26:57.840 --> 00:27:01.020
So often the analyses
are done in R,

00:27:01.020 --> 00:27:03.720
so it should play well
with the tidyverse.

00:27:03.720 --> 00:27:07.020
The plotting should probably
be done using ggplot,

00:27:07.020 --> 00:27:10.770
and then it should be rendered
in different output formats

00:27:10.770 --> 00:27:14.340
like R Markdown using,
like, HTML output as well

00:27:14.340 --> 00:27:20.130
as Word and LaTeX, or
interactively using Shiny.

00:27:20.130 --> 00:27:24.120
The package or tool box should
be suitable for our special use

00:27:24.120 --> 00:27:29.140
cases in clinical development,
but might also reach beyond.

00:27:29.140 --> 00:27:30.780
And it should be
flexible enough so

00:27:30.780 --> 00:27:34.560
that also expert users
find the tools useful

00:27:34.560 --> 00:27:39.000
and like to use them to ease
some of the coding burden

00:27:39.000 --> 00:27:41.760
and create, for example,
these additional metadata

00:27:41.760 --> 00:27:46.520
objects around the visual output
they are already creating.

00:27:46.520 --> 00:27:48.320
As Mark said, the
audience might actually

00:27:48.320 --> 00:27:52.460
change for your outputs
as a project evolves.

00:27:52.460 --> 00:27:57.080
And so the tool box should
allow us to easily adapt

00:27:57.080 --> 00:27:58.970
to these different
target audiences

00:27:58.970 --> 00:28:02.060
without having to repeat
the actual analysis.

00:28:02.060 --> 00:28:04.310
And that could be, for
example, by exploring

00:28:04.310 --> 00:28:07.130
different visualizations
of the same data set

00:28:07.130 --> 00:28:11.180
and see which one helps us
adhere to those through three

00:28:11.180 --> 00:28:14.150
laws that Mark introduced,
especially which

00:28:14.150 --> 00:28:17.930
one helps us to convey the
message clearly and help

00:28:17.930 --> 00:28:20.270
in decision making.

00:28:20.270 --> 00:28:23.750
And as I said, the outputs
might make their way

00:28:23.750 --> 00:28:26.610
through different stages
of stakeholders as well.

00:28:26.610 --> 00:28:28.550
So it should be
possible to export them.

00:28:28.550 --> 00:28:29.920
And there was something very--

00:28:29.920 --> 00:28:31.910
being very critical
from our teammates

00:28:31.910 --> 00:28:36.620
in the therapeutic area side
so that you can output them,

00:28:36.620 --> 00:28:40.010
for example, so that they
can easily take the output

00:28:40.010 --> 00:28:43.702
and present it in their
own presentations.

00:28:43.702 --> 00:28:45.160
And so if you go
to the next slide.

00:28:48.140 --> 00:28:52.460
This quickly led us to the
idea of developing an R package

00:28:52.460 --> 00:28:55.490
because R is increasingly
popular and widely used

00:28:55.490 --> 00:28:56.815
in our teams.

00:28:56.815 --> 00:28:58.190
And it already
comes with a bunch

00:28:58.190 --> 00:29:01.010
of really excellent
existing packages that

00:29:01.010 --> 00:29:04.130
solve parts of the problems
that we just stated.

00:29:04.130 --> 00:29:06.320
So there are
packages already that

00:29:06.320 --> 00:29:08.527
allow very flexible
plotting opportunities.

00:29:08.527 --> 00:29:09.360
For example, ggplot.

00:29:12.080 --> 00:29:15.835
And we thought actually
building on those packages

00:29:15.835 --> 00:29:17.460
and standing on the
shoulders of giants

00:29:17.460 --> 00:29:22.410
here will make the
work less hard for us.

00:29:22.410 --> 00:29:24.540
The other nice
part about using R

00:29:24.540 --> 00:29:27.930
is that it is flexible towards
multiple analysis questions

00:29:27.930 --> 00:29:29.680
and stages of the workflow.

00:29:29.680 --> 00:29:33.600
And if we develop the package
that had different functions,

00:29:33.600 --> 00:29:35.760
we could actually build
on the flexibility that

00:29:35.760 --> 00:29:38.880
was so requested by R users.

00:29:38.880 --> 00:29:42.030
We obviously can have the
documentation and testing.

00:29:42.030 --> 00:29:44.220
And given that it's
open source, it's

00:29:44.220 --> 00:29:47.400
really easy to collaborate
across companies and even

00:29:47.400 --> 00:29:50.503
industries in the future
and contribute content.

00:29:50.503 --> 00:29:51.670
If you go to the next slide.

00:29:55.030 --> 00:30:00.870
So that's why we are now
presenting the visR package

00:30:00.870 --> 00:30:03.810
in its current form, which
is actually is a still very

00:30:03.810 --> 00:30:05.950
early experimental phase--

00:30:05.950 --> 00:30:09.030
or you could actually call
it a prototype, maybe--

00:30:09.030 --> 00:30:12.780
which we developed and would
like to hear feedback of you

00:30:12.780 --> 00:30:14.430
all later on, as well.

00:30:14.430 --> 00:30:19.350
We thought, too, there are a
few architecture considerations

00:30:19.350 --> 00:30:23.670
we put in in order to
implement those considerations

00:30:23.670 --> 00:30:26.850
and requirements that we got
in our earlier stakeholder

00:30:26.850 --> 00:30:27.960
interviews.

00:30:27.960 --> 00:30:31.740
So the first one was really
about this seamless integration

00:30:31.740 --> 00:30:34.150
into the tidyverse in
our analysis workflow.

00:30:34.150 --> 00:30:36.900
So we thought whatever
we do, it should interact

00:30:36.900 --> 00:30:39.600
with key player and
the modeling packages,

00:30:39.600 --> 00:30:41.850
and plotting should
be built on ggplot.

00:30:41.850 --> 00:30:43.860
We also want to keep
full transparency

00:30:43.860 --> 00:30:47.670
on data modifications, first
of all, for the reproducible

00:30:47.670 --> 00:30:53.010
reporting aspect, but also for
being able to scale projects

00:30:53.010 --> 00:30:55.410
across different stages.

00:30:55.410 --> 00:30:57.540
And that led us to
this decoupled design

00:30:57.540 --> 00:31:00.330
that you can see on the
right where we actually

00:31:00.330 --> 00:31:01.600
take our input data--

00:31:01.600 --> 00:31:04.350
let's say, for example,
one row per sample,

00:31:04.350 --> 00:31:06.510
one row per patient data set.

00:31:06.510 --> 00:31:08.430
And then we have
dedicated functions

00:31:08.430 --> 00:31:12.180
that take care of the data
wrangling and the modeling

00:31:12.180 --> 00:31:15.300
before another set
of functions takes

00:31:15.300 --> 00:31:18.460
care of the visualization.

00:31:18.460 --> 00:31:20.480
And that visualization
ideally adheres

00:31:20.480 --> 00:31:24.930
to the graphical principles
that Mark just described before.

00:31:24.930 --> 00:31:27.680
And then last but not least,
we have a separate set

00:31:27.680 --> 00:31:32.360
of functions, again,
that takes care

00:31:32.360 --> 00:31:35.100
of the styling of the outputs.

00:31:35.100 --> 00:31:37.738
So there shouldn't be any hard
coded theming, for example,

00:31:37.738 --> 00:31:38.780
in the plotting function.

00:31:38.780 --> 00:31:42.090
But that is then taken care
of and allows us, for example,

00:31:42.090 --> 00:31:45.540
to adapt the visualization
to corporate designs.

00:31:45.540 --> 00:31:48.230
But it also allows us to
adapt the visualization

00:31:48.230 --> 00:31:51.630
to be consistent
across a report where,

00:31:51.630 --> 00:31:55.755
for example, the treatments are
always colored in the same way

00:31:55.755 --> 00:31:56.255
across.

00:31:59.000 --> 00:32:02.480
We mentioned before as well that
in addition to being flexible,

00:32:02.480 --> 00:32:05.060
though, the coding
of and adhering

00:32:05.060 --> 00:32:08.990
to those graphical principles
shouldn't add a lot of overhead

00:32:08.990 --> 00:32:12.830
to the coding work you're doing
as a data scientist anyways.

00:32:12.830 --> 00:32:14.480
So we thought it
would be great to have

00:32:14.480 --> 00:32:18.190
in addition to those
individual decoupled functions

00:32:18.190 --> 00:32:20.300
the wrapper functions
that allow you

00:32:20.300 --> 00:32:22.130
to explore different
visualizations

00:32:22.130 --> 00:32:24.830
for typical analysis questions.

00:32:24.830 --> 00:32:27.740
So one, to pick a question
in clinical development

00:32:27.740 --> 00:32:31.100
when working with a patient
cohort in the beginning

00:32:31.100 --> 00:32:33.860
is, for example, to see how many
of those patients in the data

00:32:33.860 --> 00:32:37.930
set adhere to a set of inclusion
and exclusion criteria.

00:32:37.930 --> 00:32:41.428
And you might want to
show the cohort attrition.

00:32:41.428 --> 00:32:43.220
And there are different
ways of doing that,

00:32:43.220 --> 00:32:45.350
and Diego will talk
about it a bit later.

00:32:45.350 --> 00:32:47.713
But having a wrapper
function that

00:32:47.713 --> 00:32:49.880
allows you to explore these
different visualizations

00:32:49.880 --> 00:32:52.460
can then help you
quickly find out

00:32:52.460 --> 00:32:55.010
which visualization
might be the best

00:32:55.010 --> 00:32:56.540
one to convey your
message clearly

00:32:56.540 --> 00:32:58.580
to the target audience
you're currently

00:32:58.580 --> 00:33:01.040
designing your report for.

00:33:01.040 --> 00:33:04.300
If you go to the next slide.

00:33:04.300 --> 00:33:08.170
So this was all very
theoretical so far.

00:33:08.170 --> 00:33:09.850
And it's still
going to stay a bit

00:33:09.850 --> 00:33:11.320
theoretical for
the next two slides

00:33:11.320 --> 00:33:13.390
before I hand over to Diego.

00:33:13.390 --> 00:33:17.140
But what I want to
show here is really

00:33:17.140 --> 00:33:20.190
how this would look
if you implemented it

00:33:20.190 --> 00:33:22.870
for a typical question,
another typical question,

00:33:22.870 --> 00:33:25.330
around time-to-event analysis.

00:33:25.330 --> 00:33:27.080
And so by having
decoupled functions,

00:33:27.080 --> 00:33:31.810
if we had our initial input
data model for patient data

00:33:31.810 --> 00:33:35.460
that has, for example,
intervention like treatment,

00:33:35.460 --> 00:33:38.440
and then the time of
followup or time to event,

00:33:38.440 --> 00:33:41.410
and the status at
the end, then we

00:33:41.410 --> 00:33:46.430
wouldn't go directly from this
input data model to the plot.

00:33:46.430 --> 00:33:48.770
But we would go through
three individual steps.

00:33:48.770 --> 00:33:51.640
The first one is really
taking this input data

00:33:51.640 --> 00:33:53.770
and have an estimator
function that

00:33:53.770 --> 00:33:57.880
computes the estimate, in
this case, the survival model.

00:33:57.880 --> 00:34:01.060
And after cleaning
that with broom,

00:34:01.060 --> 00:34:04.420
we get a data
table, or a tibble,

00:34:04.420 --> 00:34:07.780
as an interim data
model, which we can then

00:34:07.780 --> 00:34:09.780
build our visualizations on.

00:34:09.780 --> 00:34:11.319
And in this case,
for example, it

00:34:11.319 --> 00:34:14.110
would contain the
time, the stratum,

00:34:14.110 --> 00:34:16.719
as well as the estimates
at different time points,

00:34:16.719 --> 00:34:18.429
and the confidence intervals.

00:34:18.429 --> 00:34:20.320
And these can then
be easily passed on

00:34:20.320 --> 00:34:24.370
to ggplot, for example,
for visualization.

00:34:24.370 --> 00:34:26.860
As I said before, we didn't
want to reinvent the wheel

00:34:26.860 --> 00:34:28.600
where this wasn't necessary.

00:34:28.600 --> 00:34:31.000
So we are building a
lot of those functions

00:34:31.000 --> 00:34:35.739
on existing packages of
the tidyverse and beyond,

00:34:35.739 --> 00:34:39.780
such as broom, or in
this case, also Survival.

00:34:39.780 --> 00:34:43.060
The idea is by having this
multi-tier approach that you

00:34:43.060 --> 00:34:46.179
can define a lot of
custom features that

00:34:46.179 --> 00:34:49.690
might be required, such
as custom time windows

00:34:49.690 --> 00:34:52.770
or adding hypothesis tests.

00:34:52.770 --> 00:34:57.080
And so after you have
this interim data model,

00:34:57.080 --> 00:34:59.370
you'd have the visualization
function, and then

00:34:59.370 --> 00:35:01.470
the styling in
the end that helps

00:35:01.470 --> 00:35:04.230
you allow to adhere to
those graphical principles

00:35:04.230 --> 00:35:06.560
that we spoke a
bit about before.

00:35:06.560 --> 00:35:08.370
And so if you go
to the next slide.

00:35:13.590 --> 00:35:14.090
Mark.

00:35:17.000 --> 00:35:18.530
Mark, can you go
to the next slide?

00:35:18.530 --> 00:35:19.620
Or is it stuck just when we--

00:35:19.620 --> 00:35:20.120
OK.

00:35:20.120 --> 00:35:21.620
Thank you.

00:35:21.620 --> 00:35:26.450
So these were a lot of
the design considerations

00:35:26.450 --> 00:35:28.920
that are definitely
not set in stone.

00:35:28.920 --> 00:35:30.980
So if people have ideas
and thoughts about it,

00:35:30.980 --> 00:35:32.510
we would love to hear them.

00:35:32.510 --> 00:35:34.970
But Diego will show you
now in the next few slides

00:35:34.970 --> 00:35:39.050
how we implemented the prototype
and what those outputs look

00:35:39.050 --> 00:35:40.265
like for now.

00:35:40.265 --> 00:35:45.347
And with that I would like
to hand over to you, Diego.

00:35:45.347 --> 00:35:48.660
DIEGO SALDANA:
Thanks, Charlotta.

00:35:48.660 --> 00:35:50.140
Yeah.

00:35:50.140 --> 00:35:52.690
So now that Charlotta
has introduced

00:35:52.690 --> 00:35:56.050
the main architecture
considerations

00:35:56.050 --> 00:36:00.380
and the main concepts that
we used to design a package,

00:36:00.380 --> 00:36:02.140
let's look at this
in the context

00:36:02.140 --> 00:36:04.250
of a practical application.

00:36:04.250 --> 00:36:07.240
So let's look at this in
the context of a time--

00:36:07.240 --> 00:36:10.540
your typical time-to-event
analysis workflow.

00:36:10.540 --> 00:36:15.280
And so when we're carrying
out these types of analyses,

00:36:15.280 --> 00:36:18.520
there's a number of
steps that will be there

00:36:18.520 --> 00:36:22.000
virtually every time we
carry out these analyses.

00:36:22.000 --> 00:36:28.150
And all of which require
some level of communicating

00:36:28.150 --> 00:36:31.810
results or communicating
interim results, and which

00:36:31.810 --> 00:36:34.900
can be communicated in different
ways depending on the audience

00:36:34.900 --> 00:36:36.700
and depending on
the circumstances as

00:36:36.700 --> 00:36:40.977
mentioned also by Mark before.

00:36:40.977 --> 00:36:43.060
And so for example, you
might start with something

00:36:43.060 --> 00:36:44.690
like building your
analysis cohorts.

00:36:44.690 --> 00:36:46.732
So you might want to
understand how many patients

00:36:46.732 --> 00:36:50.380
are kept after applying certain
inclusion/exclusion criteria.

00:36:50.380 --> 00:36:52.630
And you might want to show
that by either showing

00:36:52.630 --> 00:36:54.075
a table or a flow chart.

00:36:56.620 --> 00:36:58.780
Then once you have
your analysis cohort,

00:36:58.780 --> 00:37:02.560
you might want to understand
their baseline characteristics

00:37:02.560 --> 00:37:05.020
of the population
that you're analyzing.

00:37:05.020 --> 00:37:07.270
And so you might do that
using some kind of Table One,

00:37:07.270 --> 00:37:11.710
which is a very popular table
that's used in virtually--

00:37:11.710 --> 00:37:13.930
well, in virtually
every paper out there

00:37:13.930 --> 00:37:16.240
on these types of studies.

00:37:16.240 --> 00:37:19.600
And then you might be
interested in doing

00:37:19.600 --> 00:37:21.520
some kind of survival analysis.

00:37:21.520 --> 00:37:23.740
And for that you
might plot something

00:37:23.740 --> 00:37:26.570
like a Kaplan-Meier
plot, or you might

00:37:26.570 --> 00:37:31.547
want to show some tables
showing the median survival.

00:37:31.547 --> 00:37:33.130
So there are different
things that you

00:37:33.130 --> 00:37:36.640
might want to show
as interim results

00:37:36.640 --> 00:37:41.350
and that all require a certain
level of code and programming

00:37:41.350 --> 00:37:43.503
and effort and time.

00:37:43.503 --> 00:37:44.920
And so we as data
scientists spend

00:37:44.920 --> 00:37:50.890
a lot of time redoing these
a lot of very often, right.

00:37:50.890 --> 00:37:54.180
So can we go to
next slide, please?

00:37:54.180 --> 00:37:56.260
So now let's look
at these one by one.

00:37:56.260 --> 00:37:59.630
So the first one was
building an analysis cohort.

00:37:59.630 --> 00:38:00.700
Right.

00:38:00.700 --> 00:38:03.270
And so first, the
philosophy that we

00:38:03.270 --> 00:38:07.110
have tried to
follow with visR was

00:38:07.110 --> 00:38:09.810
that we wanted to, as
Charlotta mentioned,

00:38:09.810 --> 00:38:11.660
not create more work.

00:38:11.660 --> 00:38:15.420
But we also wanted
this to implement sort

00:38:15.420 --> 00:38:19.380
of out-of-the-box best practices
and also reuse the knowledge

00:38:19.380 --> 00:38:23.340
that we have acquired through
our own experience and through

00:38:23.340 --> 00:38:26.220
interviews that we
have carried out.

00:38:26.220 --> 00:38:30.190
And so the way this
works, as you can see,

00:38:30.190 --> 00:38:35.340
you can create a list of
filters as well as descriptions.

00:38:35.340 --> 00:38:39.150
So it takes very little code
to create your cohort attrition

00:38:39.150 --> 00:38:40.710
using this visR.

00:38:40.710 --> 00:38:46.440
And then really creating a table
that shows the attrition just

00:38:46.440 --> 00:38:51.300
takes basically five lines
of code using this function

00:38:51.300 --> 00:38:54.270
called vr_render_table.

00:38:54.270 --> 00:38:57.540
And then you can output this
nicely formatted table that

00:38:57.540 --> 00:39:01.170
shows you the relevant
information, including

00:39:01.170 --> 00:39:03.600
the criteria that's
being applied,

00:39:03.600 --> 00:39:05.310
what's the condition,
how many patients

00:39:05.310 --> 00:39:08.970
are being kept and excluded, as
well as percentages for these.

00:39:08.970 --> 00:39:11.100
And of course, it
comes with nice--

00:39:11.100 --> 00:39:14.340
I mean, it will show nicely
formatted titles as well as

00:39:14.340 --> 00:39:17.802
metadata that might be
relevant, such as data sources.

00:39:17.802 --> 00:39:20.010
And so you don't have to
spend a lot of time thinking

00:39:20.010 --> 00:39:22.770
of what should I show to the--

00:39:22.770 --> 00:39:25.110
what should I show
in this plot in order

00:39:25.110 --> 00:39:27.180
to follow best practices.

00:39:27.180 --> 00:39:30.780
Again, little code, very
little code, and nicely

00:39:30.780 --> 00:39:36.260
formatted outputs follows best
practices and reuses knowledge.

00:39:36.260 --> 00:39:37.430
Can we go to the next slide?

00:39:41.040 --> 00:39:42.697
So then you might
also want to show

00:39:42.697 --> 00:39:44.280
this in the form of
a flowchart, which

00:39:44.280 --> 00:39:48.760
is a very typical visualization
of the attrition diagram.

00:39:48.760 --> 00:39:50.970
And so, again, this
takes very little code.

00:39:50.970 --> 00:39:54.390
You can create a vector
of complement descriptions

00:39:54.390 --> 00:39:57.870
as well as reduce the outputs
from the previous chunk

00:39:57.870 --> 00:39:58.920
of code.

00:39:58.920 --> 00:40:01.860
And using this
vr_attrition function,

00:40:01.860 --> 00:40:05.850
you can plot this nicely
formatted attrition diagram,

00:40:05.850 --> 00:40:07.860
which shows at each
step how many patients

00:40:07.860 --> 00:40:11.110
have been kept and excluded.

00:40:11.110 --> 00:40:13.860
And this is a very
popular, again,

00:40:13.860 --> 00:40:18.840
figure that shows up in many
real world data analyses

00:40:18.840 --> 00:40:21.240
papers.

00:40:21.240 --> 00:40:25.290
And I should highlight
that this type of package

00:40:25.290 --> 00:40:28.890
can help us better comply
with reporting guidelines

00:40:28.890 --> 00:40:31.860
like CONSORT and STROBE.

00:40:31.860 --> 00:40:35.890
Can we go to the
next slide, please.

00:40:35.890 --> 00:40:39.580
So once we have selected
our baseline population

00:40:39.580 --> 00:40:43.010
and our analyses
cohort, we might

00:40:43.010 --> 00:40:44.760
be interested in showing
and understanding

00:40:44.760 --> 00:40:46.500
the baseline characteristics.

00:40:46.500 --> 00:40:49.680
And so this is typically done
using the very popular Table

00:40:49.680 --> 00:40:51.210
One.

00:40:51.210 --> 00:40:53.610
We looked at a number of
implementations of it,

00:40:53.610 --> 00:40:56.850
and we couldn't find one
that really satisfied all

00:40:56.850 --> 00:40:59.980
the requirements that we had.

00:40:59.980 --> 00:41:03.090
So we have created our own.

00:41:03.090 --> 00:41:06.480
The nice thing about it is
that it's very flexible.

00:41:06.480 --> 00:41:12.390
So it comes already with a very
good inbuilt summary functions

00:41:12.390 --> 00:41:15.510
that will help you summarize
your categorical as well

00:41:15.510 --> 00:41:19.140
as numerical variables
out of the box.

00:41:19.140 --> 00:41:22.980
But it also lets you input
your own custom built

00:41:22.980 --> 00:41:24.960
summary functions if
they don't satisfy

00:41:24.960 --> 00:41:27.720
your specific requirement.

00:41:27.720 --> 00:41:30.700
It also outputs the tables
in different formats,

00:41:30.700 --> 00:41:35.910
including kable, RStudio/gt,
as well as DT html tables.

00:41:35.910 --> 00:41:39.690
And importantly, it also lets
you download the Table One

00:41:39.690 --> 00:41:45.500
at Excel or CSV, which was
one of the requests that

00:41:45.500 --> 00:41:47.720
came the most often
when we interviewed

00:41:47.720 --> 00:41:51.290
our internal scientists.

00:41:51.290 --> 00:41:52.970
And again, it takes
very little code.

00:41:52.970 --> 00:41:57.170
It's about four lines
of code, and again,

00:41:57.170 --> 00:42:01.280
follows best practices
and reuses the knowledge

00:42:01.280 --> 00:42:02.380
that we have acquired.

00:42:02.380 --> 00:42:03.380
Can we go to next slide.

00:42:06.260 --> 00:42:11.682
So finally, we might be
interested-- or actually, next.

00:42:11.682 --> 00:42:13.140
We might be interested
in-- no, no.

00:42:13.140 --> 00:42:13.580
Sorry.

00:42:13.580 --> 00:42:14.200
It's the same.

00:42:14.200 --> 00:42:15.130
Yeah.

00:42:15.130 --> 00:42:19.960
We might be interested in
doing a survival analysis.

00:42:19.960 --> 00:42:25.840
So this is typically done using
a Kaplan-Meier curve together

00:42:25.840 --> 00:42:28.930
with a risk table.

00:42:28.930 --> 00:42:34.330
And usually there's a
number of metadata elements

00:42:34.330 --> 00:42:36.735
that are important here.

00:42:36.735 --> 00:42:38.110
And so I should
highlight that we

00:42:38.110 --> 00:42:42.970
have based our design of our
Kaplan-Meier visualization

00:42:42.970 --> 00:42:46.630
on the outcome and the
findings of a paper

00:42:46.630 --> 00:42:50.080
by Morris and collaborators
that was conducted

00:42:50.080 --> 00:42:51.670
among a large number
of researchers

00:42:51.670 --> 00:42:56.120
about what a good Kaplan-Meier
plot should look like.

00:42:56.120 --> 00:43:01.340
And so our Kaplan-Meier plot
shows relevant information out

00:43:01.340 --> 00:43:04.790
of the box, including things
like number of patients,

00:43:04.790 --> 00:43:08.930
nicely formatted axis labels
including units where needed,

00:43:08.930 --> 00:43:14.360
as well as a data source
from which the plot was made.

00:43:14.360 --> 00:43:18.770
It also shows the risk table
in a format including things

00:43:18.770 --> 00:43:22.010
like numbers, number of patients
at risk, number of events,

00:43:22.010 --> 00:43:25.960
and number of censoring events
as well grouped by stratum

00:43:25.960 --> 00:43:27.320
at regular time points.

00:43:27.320 --> 00:43:29.840
And this format actually
follows the recommendations

00:43:29.840 --> 00:43:31.580
by Morris and collaborators.

00:43:31.580 --> 00:43:35.680
So again, this is reusing
knowledge and best s

00:43:35.680 --> 00:43:37.150
Can we go to next slide?

00:43:40.090 --> 00:43:42.250
So how do we do this?

00:43:42.250 --> 00:43:44.410
Using visR, it's very simple.

00:43:44.410 --> 00:43:45.410
We have two options.

00:43:45.410 --> 00:43:49.450
One is either we plot the
Kaplan-Meier curve alone

00:43:49.450 --> 00:43:53.710
using the first chunk here and
then the risk table alone using

00:43:53.710 --> 00:43:56.410
the second chunk
here in the middle.

00:43:56.410 --> 00:44:01.060
And both of these show
our design philosophy

00:44:01.060 --> 00:44:03.190
of separating the
estimation from the plotting

00:44:03.190 --> 00:44:05.440
and the styling.

00:44:05.440 --> 00:44:08.920
But then we also have
the option of plotting

00:44:08.920 --> 00:44:11.020
all of this in one go
and one function call.

00:44:11.020 --> 00:44:14.300
And it's the chunk
in the bottom here.

00:44:14.300 --> 00:44:16.360
So this one is just
one function call,

00:44:16.360 --> 00:44:19.330
and then gives you
that nice plot.

00:44:19.330 --> 00:44:24.750
Again, very little code, reuses
best practices and knowledge.

00:44:24.750 --> 00:44:25.750
Can we go to next slide?

00:44:28.850 --> 00:44:30.580
So finally, we have
also implemented

00:44:30.580 --> 00:44:33.670
a set of convenient
estimation functions,

00:44:33.670 --> 00:44:37.210
including median
survival times by stratum

00:44:37.210 --> 00:44:39.160
as well as multiple
methods for testing

00:44:39.160 --> 00:44:41.200
equality between strata.

00:44:41.200 --> 00:44:42.970
And again, you can
do this very easily

00:44:42.970 --> 00:44:44.560
with only a few lines of code.

00:44:44.560 --> 00:44:47.920
And it shows you very
nicely formatted tables

00:44:47.920 --> 00:44:49.600
that follow best practices.

00:44:49.600 --> 00:44:50.790
Can we go to next slide?

00:44:56.340 --> 00:44:59.670
So now going into
our package roadmap,

00:44:59.670 --> 00:45:04.320
I would like to highlight that
this is still a prototype.

00:45:04.320 --> 00:45:07.425
So we are basically
reaching now the stage

00:45:07.425 --> 00:45:12.990
where we have our prototype
that's reaching the state

00:45:12.990 --> 00:45:14.350
where it's ready.

00:45:14.350 --> 00:45:16.620
And now we are
reaching the stage

00:45:16.620 --> 00:45:21.300
where we want to launch this
initiative out there and find

00:45:21.300 --> 00:45:26.970
and identify
collaborators to kickstart

00:45:26.970 --> 00:45:30.660
the next step of our projects.

00:45:30.660 --> 00:45:33.528
Can we go to next slide, please?

00:45:33.528 --> 00:45:35.070
And so with that
I'd like to announce

00:45:35.070 --> 00:45:37.290
that we're looking for
contributors to join the visR

00:45:37.290 --> 00:45:38.880
team.

00:45:38.880 --> 00:45:43.500
So again, visR is still in
its experimental stages.

00:45:43.500 --> 00:45:48.360
We welcome feedback and
ideas for features using

00:45:48.360 --> 00:45:50.082
GitHub issues, for example.

00:45:50.082 --> 00:45:52.540
So actually there are two ways
in which you can contribute.

00:45:52.540 --> 00:45:56.910
The first one is what we
call the open source way.

00:45:56.910 --> 00:45:58.660
And so this is the
informal collaboration,

00:45:58.660 --> 00:46:02.453
where you might pick up an issue
and work on it proactively.

00:46:02.453 --> 00:46:04.620
The second way it might be
the more formal way where

00:46:04.620 --> 00:46:07.380
you reach out to us, and then
we can see together if it makes

00:46:07.380 --> 00:46:10.480
sense for you to try
to join our core team.

00:46:10.480 --> 00:46:12.120
And so what kind
of contributions

00:46:12.120 --> 00:46:13.180
are we looking for?

00:46:13.180 --> 00:46:15.310
So we're open to
hearing your ideas

00:46:15.310 --> 00:46:19.350
in turn and ask for design
choices, project governance,

00:46:19.350 --> 00:46:21.660
hands-on engineering,
as well as how

00:46:21.660 --> 00:46:24.540
to maintain an
actively used package.

00:46:24.540 --> 00:46:27.810
And if you want to reach
out, please reach out

00:46:27.810 --> 00:46:32.553
to Mark Baille and James Black,
whose emails are listed here.

00:46:32.553 --> 00:46:33.720
Can we go to the next slide?

00:46:36.780 --> 00:46:41.910
Finally, I'd like to acknowledge
that many other people have

00:46:41.910 --> 00:46:44.530
been involved in the
development of this package.

00:46:44.530 --> 00:46:47.160
So we would like to thank them.

00:46:47.160 --> 00:46:51.210
And also, we have made heavy use
of open source packages, which

00:46:51.210 --> 00:46:55.770
are listed here, without
which this prototype would not

00:46:55.770 --> 00:46:58.570
have been possible to develop.

00:46:58.570 --> 00:47:02.340
And with that, I'd like
to hand over to Marc.

00:47:04.032 --> 00:47:05.240
MARC VANDEMEULEBROECKE: Yeah.

00:47:05.240 --> 00:47:06.800
So thanks, Diego.

00:47:06.800 --> 00:47:09.620
And thanks, Mark and Charlotta.

00:47:09.620 --> 00:47:11.750
That's the end of
the presentation.

00:47:11.750 --> 00:47:16.220
And I guess we can
start the Q&amp;amp;A session.

00:47:16.220 --> 00:47:19.220
Let me start with one of
the questions that came in

00:47:19.220 --> 00:47:21.680
during the presentations.

00:47:21.680 --> 00:47:25.640
Someone has asked, are you
going to open collaborations

00:47:25.640 --> 00:47:28.580
with federal agencies?

00:47:28.580 --> 00:47:34.250
And we are working with
various other groups.

00:47:34.250 --> 00:47:36.290
But more specifically,
maybe Mark,

00:47:36.290 --> 00:47:39.870
do you want to
take this question?

00:47:39.870 --> 00:47:40.620
MARK BAILLE: Yeah.

00:47:40.620 --> 00:47:41.840
Thanks, Marc.

00:47:41.840 --> 00:47:45.570
We would be open to
collaborating with anyone.

00:47:45.570 --> 00:47:49.450
This is part of a wider
set of collaborations.

00:47:49.450 --> 00:47:52.300
So for example, the PSI--

00:47:52.300 --> 00:47:57.270
the Pharmaceutical
Statistics Initiative--

00:47:57.270 --> 00:47:59.970
has sort of started a
special interest group

00:47:59.970 --> 00:48:02.790
on data visualization
where a number of us

00:48:02.790 --> 00:48:06.060
are currently joint members of.

00:48:06.060 --> 00:48:08.483
And also, there's the
Stratus Collaboration,

00:48:08.483 --> 00:48:09.900
which is related
to this, which is

00:48:09.900 --> 00:48:13.880
looking at methodology as a
whole for observational data.

00:48:13.880 --> 00:48:15.930
But yes, we would be
open to collaborations

00:48:15.930 --> 00:48:17.520
from any avenues.

00:48:21.440 --> 00:48:24.080
Thank you, Mark.

00:48:24.080 --> 00:48:26.150
Another question
that came in earlier

00:48:26.150 --> 00:48:30.747
was, when will this
package be on [? Crayon? ?]

00:48:30.747 --> 00:48:31.580
DIEGO SALDANA: Yeah.

00:48:31.580 --> 00:48:32.700
Yeah.

00:48:32.700 --> 00:48:36.350
So as I said currently,
