00:48:36.350 --> 00:48:40.910
I think we would still
have to probably go further

00:48:40.910 --> 00:48:44.990
into development in order
to put it into [? Crayon. ?]

00:48:44.990 --> 00:48:47.450
But definitely we have
it in our radar to do.

00:48:50.365 --> 00:48:53.500
MARC VANDEMEULEBROECKE:
[INAUDIBLE] Thank you.

00:48:53.500 --> 00:48:55.000
Another question
was, what do you

00:48:55.000 --> 00:48:58.100
mean by "join the core team"?

00:48:58.100 --> 00:48:58.740
Charlotta.

00:49:01.513 --> 00:49:03.180
CHARLOTTA FRUCHTENICHT:
Thank you, Marc.

00:49:03.180 --> 00:49:06.130
"Join the core team" I guess
means that at the moment

00:49:06.130 --> 00:49:09.090
we are this relatively
small group of people

00:49:09.090 --> 00:49:14.100
who have regular meetings
around aspects like the package

00:49:14.100 --> 00:49:16.350
architecture, for example.

00:49:16.350 --> 00:49:18.750
And while you might
not want to commit

00:49:18.750 --> 00:49:23.480
to having those alignment
meetings and other aspects,

00:49:23.480 --> 00:49:25.500
I guess if you
joined the core team,

00:49:25.500 --> 00:49:29.970
this would be part of, like,
driving the general direction

00:49:29.970 --> 00:49:34.440
of the package, making design
choices, and things like that.

00:49:34.440 --> 00:49:36.450
Whereas if you just
picked an issue on GitHub

00:49:36.450 --> 00:49:39.930
and contributed to it, that
would be the less involvement

00:49:39.930 --> 00:49:41.070
in the overall direction.

00:49:44.210 --> 00:49:46.670
MARC VANDEMEULEBROECKE:
Thank you, Charlotta.

00:49:46.670 --> 00:49:50.870
So there's two questions,
which are relatively similar.

00:49:50.870 --> 00:49:55.400
One was, is this focusing
on clinical data only?

00:49:55.400 --> 00:49:58.520
And another
question, do you have

00:49:58.520 --> 00:50:01.880
a sub team focused on
reporting for real world data?

00:50:01.880 --> 00:50:04.010
So I guess overall,
the question is, what's

00:50:04.010 --> 00:50:08.420
the focus of this effort?

00:50:08.420 --> 00:50:11.650
Mark, do you want
to take that one?

00:50:11.650 --> 00:50:13.400
MARK BAILLE: I would
say the general focus

00:50:13.400 --> 00:50:16.070
at the moment is
exploratory analysis

00:50:16.070 --> 00:50:19.960
of clinical and medical data.

00:50:19.960 --> 00:50:22.680
Probably Diego and Charlotta
could back to me up here.

00:50:22.680 --> 00:50:25.820
But they work primarily
on real world data.

00:50:25.820 --> 00:50:28.400
So it is a mixture of
clinical and real world data.

00:50:32.363 --> 00:50:34.030
MARC VANDEMEULEBROECKE:
Thank you, Mark.

00:50:37.520 --> 00:50:41.480
A quick one was, can
you share the link again

00:50:41.480 --> 00:50:42.860
to those graphical principles?

00:50:42.860 --> 00:50:43.780
I will take this one.

00:50:43.780 --> 00:50:46.180
You can see it on
this last slide.

00:50:50.240 --> 00:50:54.740
And here's another one.

00:50:54.740 --> 00:50:58.460
Do you have any recommendations
for developing visualizations

00:50:58.460 --> 00:51:00.890
for different kinds of users--

00:51:00.890 --> 00:51:06.520
users with different fluency
in data, for example?

00:51:06.520 --> 00:51:09.130
Diego, what are your
thoughts on this one?

00:51:12.007 --> 00:51:12.840
DIEGO SALDANA: Yeah.

00:51:12.840 --> 00:51:19.230
I think in general, as Mark
mentioned before, it's really

00:51:19.230 --> 00:51:21.510
about knowing your audience.

00:51:21.510 --> 00:51:24.540
So I really couldn't
give you, like, one rule

00:51:24.540 --> 00:51:27.840
of thumb for everything.

00:51:27.840 --> 00:51:30.570
But we definitely need,
I think, just in--

00:51:30.570 --> 00:51:34.080
as for like how we are handling
that with the package, that's

00:51:34.080 --> 00:51:35.850
really part of why
we have decided

00:51:35.850 --> 00:51:40.170
to sort of separate the
idea of doing estimates

00:51:40.170 --> 00:51:44.910
and visualizations and having
multiple ways of presenting

00:51:44.910 --> 00:51:48.510
a message, because yeah,
as Mark said, really

00:51:48.510 --> 00:51:49.770
you should know your audience.

00:51:49.770 --> 00:51:53.210
And so that also translates
into being able to show things

00:51:53.210 --> 00:51:55.830
in different-- or being
able to communicate

00:51:55.830 --> 00:51:57.630
your results in different ways.

00:51:57.630 --> 00:51:59.130
And so that flexibility
is something

00:51:59.130 --> 00:52:01.740
that we want to embed
in our project as well.

00:52:04.342 --> 00:52:06.050
MARC VANDEMEULEBROECKE:
Thank you, Diego.

00:52:06.050 --> 00:52:12.320
I'll move on to a question
regarding compatibility.

00:52:12.320 --> 00:52:17.220
Two people have asked
about intentions

00:52:17.220 --> 00:52:22.560
to combine this with LaTeX or
somehow create a link to LaTeX.

00:52:22.560 --> 00:52:25.790
And on the other hand, whether
we are planning to create

00:52:25.790 --> 00:52:31.130
some templates for
reporting with R Markdown.

00:52:31.130 --> 00:52:35.150
So both of the questions
maybe can go to Mark.

00:52:40.550 --> 00:52:41.420
MARK BAILLE: Yeah.

